=== BLOCKER ANALYSIS (pre-existing, out-of-scope) ===

B1 — Sibling AI* surfaces share an identical pre-existing escaping-closure bug:
   pattern: `private func glass(@ViewBuilder _ content: () -> some View) -> some View { TSGlassPanel { content() ... } }`
   TSGlassPanel stores its closure as @escaping, so passing the non-escaping `content` into it is a
   hard, deterministic Swift error. Files affected (grep, all OUTSIDE allowed scope):
     - AISignalExplorerNlFilter.swift:142            (the iOS gate's determinative error)
     - AIGeofenceAwareAutomationSuggestions.swift:141
     - AISuggestNewGeofences.swift
     - AIAutoNameUnnamedLocations.swift
   count = 4. BottomTabBar.* uses no such pattern (grep => "no such pattern (clean)").
   Additional flaky sibling failures observed under parallel-slot memory pressure (transient, not in
   this surface): AppearanceSettings.Controls.swift (swift-frontend IRGen abort / report_at_maximum_capacity)
   and RecentDrivesSection.Previews.swift:24 ("unable to type-check this expression in reasonable time").

B2 — macOS code-signing: the runner has no provisioned Apple Developer identity (DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=""),
   so the macOS app + widget extension fail entitlement signing before any Swift is compiled. This is
   a platform-capability limitation of the runner, independent of source.

Neither blocker is fixable within the allowed files (BottomTabBar.* + this log). Fixing B1 would
require editing four sibling surfaces (forbidden — each owns its own prompt); B2 requires a signing
certificate on the runner.

